 up but as well as that there's  been studies done in the lab  in port and down now this isn't  published in peer-reviewed data yet but  we're getting reports  that this virus is more contagious is  more infectious  and it could be around about 70 percent  more contagious  now that's significant because that  would increase the r value  by 0.4 so it is a potentially  significant increase and it's already  spread throughout other parts of the  united kingdom in as well it's in wales  it's in scotland and people when these  restrictions were announced these t4  tier 4 restrictions were announced in  london people like fled london to all  parts of the country  so there is the potential for spread and  because it's been around since september  there's also potential but it already is  in european countries  it's just that the uk has got a  particularly high level of genomic  surveillance so  maybe it's just that we picked it up  first this is not really clear yet  but i am concerned it's more infectious  that is the concentric consensus of  medical opinion  in the uk at the moment we'll have to  leave it there john campbell independent  health analyst joining us from the uk  thank you so much for that  analysis thank you so much thank you  let's bring in our correspondent spirit  mass in london and max sander  in brussels berget britain has  effectively been blacklisted  travel from the uk to several countries  has been banned  how is britain reacting to that  well boris johnson is holding crisis  talks with his cabinet this morning in  an emergency  meeting and they need to establish how  exactly the  uk is affected there is one concern  which  is food supply because it's not only air  travel it's not only passengers that  can't go  but it's also um lorries that cannot go  to france and we know that there are  already  um huge queues so there's no  um there's no fresh produce for the  moment uh al  also coming in from france so that  obviously if that continues that would  be a concern and so the government will  be addressing that  also what does it mean for medical  supplies for example the coronavirus  vaccine there are  more batches expected here in the uk and  the government needs to solve  how that will come into the country and  it's been reported that military  airplanes are on standby  but yes those are just some of the  issues that  the cabinet will be debating this  morning  max i understand that representatives of  the eu  countries are planning to meet later to  coordinate their response  to the new strain of the virus could we  see even more restrictions imposed  here on the continent perhaps further  border closures  right terry well we have seen some  strong reactions from eu leaders from eu  countries already over the past  24 hours or so and it really does seem  that eu  and eu leaders are in panic mode right  now with  numbers really out of control and uh  this news of this new uh  strain of virus uh coming up now the eu  a council uh presidency  headed by germany has called for this  crisis meeting later today where they  will come together  discuss how to go forward on this  they will try to coordinate response as  closely as possible it's also important  important to point out  that the eu does not have any  competencies  in terms of border management in terms  of when a member state opens or closed  its borders that is  entirely up to the member states but  we've seen in the past  in the beginning of this pandemic back  in march and april  when member states individually started  to close their borders this plunged  a continent in crisis further into  crisis and the eu is quite  keen on not repeating this again but  we'll have to see what comes out of the  meeting for exact measures  berget boris johnson the british prime  minister he has now put  several regions under lockdown uh  and he's done this just days after his  pledge to relax rules over the christmas  period  how's that going down with the british  people  yes the government has announced that  london and  large parts of the south of england are  effectively under lockdown  and this is after boris johnson had four  months really promised that  christmas will be better even at the  beginning of the pandemic he has said  that everything will be normal by  christmas  that's a promise that he can't keep at  all  what's interesting is that he has come  under  a lot of criticism pressure even from  his own back benches he's always  promised this sort of magical moment  uh at christmas that people would be um  that would people could be seeing their  loved ones and now  really at the last minute the government  has canceled that and  the prime minister as recently as last  week the middle of last week he  ridiculed people who were saying  the virus is out of control there needs  to be tougher restrictions  and he was he was ridiculing them for  wanting to cancel christmas and just  two three days later he has to be doing  he has to do exactly that  so a lot of promises when it comes to  coronavirus and christmas that he can't  keep and that's why a lot of british  people are very very  disappointed by this

